  "executive_briefing": "The mid-2026 corporate tax and trade policy landscape is marked by a dynamic shift toward strict domestic enforcement, unilateral trade measures, and the active operationalization of international agreements. In corporate taxation, the OECD's Pillar Two global minimum tax has successfully transitioned from conceptual planning to active statutory implementation, with over 140 jurisdictions enforcing GloBE and Qualified Domestic Minimum Top-up Tax (QDMTT) rules. Domestically, companies are preparing for scheduled US tax rate increases under GILTI and FDII provisions alongside shifting European statutory tax rates in Germany, Portugal, Malta, and Italy. Regionally, Southeast Asia remains highly competitive, anchored by Singapore's 17% single-tier corporate rate. Concurrently, indirect tax compliance has evolved significantly, with Singapore executing mandatory phases of its IMDA-backed InvoiceNow network for real-time validation, and tax authorities like India expanding their tax bases via new GST impositions on intercompany corporate guarantees. On the trade front, the corporate sphere is adjusting to geopolitical friction stemming from US proposals for a 12.5% unilateral tariff targeting 60 nations over forced labor concerns.",
